# morning
## The twenty first
You sleep through it. It is found out at seven, when a valet goes in with the chocolate and sees that the bed has not been slept in, and by eight the whole of Paris has heard, and you lie in the haven, in the dark, with the stone over you, and hear none of it. You wake at dusk to a city that has been turned over like a mattress.
It is told you by the apprentice, and the baker, and a woman at a stall, all at once, in the street, and by the very walls. The bells rang, first, from every tower, the tocsin, and then the drums beat the générale, and the cannon of the Invalides fired the alarm, and a roar came up from the quays that people say was like a very large thing turning over. The king is gone. The queen is gone. The children are gone. The great gates of the Tuileries stood open all the morning, and a crowd walked into the royal apartments and sat on the beds. Somebody put a tricolor cap on the bust of Louis the Fourteenth. A seamstress took a ribbon from the queen's dressing table, as a keepsake, and cried. There is a bill pinned to the gate, that says that whoever applauds the king will be beaten, and whoever insults him will be hanged.
?if b3.flight == "aided"
  You hear it said, again and again, in the street, that the general's carriage was held up on the Carrousel for the matter of four minutes. You hear it from a baker, and from an old woman with a basket, and from a member of the Assembly, who says that it was no doubt an accident. Nobody looks at you. You walk home with your collar up and your hands in your pockets, and by the Pont Neuf, you find that your hands are shaking.
?elif b3.flight == "warned"
  You are summoned, at nine that night, to give an account. They are civil. They write it down. A young man with a pen says, with a pleasant smile, that it is a very great pity that his excellency did not feel able to credit it. It is the thing that you will hear said most often, in the next year, about a great many people.
?end
?if b3.republic == "printed"
  The sheets have been on every wall since nine in the morning. The word is a great red hand in the middle of the rue Saint-Jacques. There is a crowd round it, in the lamplight, reading it aloud. A man in a cap says that it is a very strong word. A woman says that she does not see why it should not be. They do not know where it came from. A tall boy of twenty with ink on his forehead is standing at the corner, with his hands in his pockets, a little pale, looking at the wall as at a face.
?elif b3.republic == "neutral"
  The plain sheet sold all day. It was the best seller in the quarter. It has no opinions in it, and a great deal of the truth, and nobody, in the course of that long day, forgave it.
?end
?if b3.coup == "backed"
  There is a thing that you did in the night that you do not look at. You have, in your pocket, a small gray glove, that was given to you by a man in the Prince's hall. He said that it was the Prince's, and that you would know what to do with it. You do not. You put it in a drawer.
?elif b3.coup == "stopped"
  The house in the faubourg Saint-Germain has kept its doors. You walk past it at ten that night, in the dark. It looks exactly as it always did.
?end
?if b3.garnier == "sheltered"
  He is still in the quarry. He sends up a note by the apprentice. It is a half sheet, in a clerkly hand, that says that he will not forget it.
?elif b3.garnier == "bargained"
  The files were left at noon, in a hamper, by a hackney coach. They are very complete. They are a good deal more complete than you expected.
?elif b3.garnier == "turned"
  There is a rumor in the Latin quarter, at nightfall, that the Section have found an inspector of the old police hanging from a lamp in the rue Mouffetard. It is not true. They have only found his hat.
?end
They bring the king back on the twenty fifth. He has got as far as Varennes, a little town in the Argonne, where a postmaster named Drouet knew him by the face on an assignat. He has been stopped at a bridge, in the night, by a cart of furniture, and he comes back, with his family, in the heat of the evening, in the same berline, through the whole of the city, in a silence of three hundred thousand. Nobody takes off a hat. Nobody says a word. You are told it by Adélaïde, who watched it from the roof of the shop with Étienne's spyglass. She says that she has never seen anything so loud.
-> july

# july
## The seventeenth of July
It is the Sunday. You lie through the whole of it in the cellar under the shop, and hear it only as noise, through the stone. It is, you are told, a hot, windless, brilliant day, the sort that comes to Paris once or twice in a summer, with a sky like a blue enamel plate, and a strong smell of dust. The Assembly has declared the king suspended, and then, on the sixteenth, in a kind of a panic, has restored him. It has decided that the flight was a kidnapping. It has been a quarrel of three weeks, between the Cordeliers and the Jacobins, between those who want a republic and those who want a very small king, and the Cordeliers have drawn up a petition, to be signed on the Altar of the Fatherland.
They are going to sign it today. They have gone out by thousands, with their children, in their Sunday clothes, as one goes to a fête, and they have stayed. A man sells lemonade by the steps. The great field of the Federation, that a year ago was a pit of mud, is now baked as hard as a brick, and white. There are six thousand, and then eight, and then, by the time that you go out, at the going down of the sun, perhaps ten, on the banks, and on the steps of the altar, and in the dust, signing by the light of lanterns that somebody has fetched. There are women. There are old soldiers. There are three hundred children, who have found that the banks make an excellent slide.
Baptiste is on the altar steps. He is quite easy to see, being a head taller than most, in a clean shirt, with a quill behind his ear, and a long roll of paper under his arm, among the first. He has been there since noon, so Adélaïde says. He has been asked three times by a man in a gray coat to go home.
Adélaïde has not wished him to come. She has put up the shutters. She has told Étienne that he is on no account to go out, and Étienne, with great dignity, has gone to the church of Saint-Séverin and is sitting in the north aisle, with his hat on his knee, to be seen there if anybody should ask.
Aubry is in the ranks. He is on the left of the line of the parish battalion, a little back, with his musket at the shoulder, in the yellow dust. The National Guard has been called out. There has been a riot, in the afternoon, at the Gros-Caillou, over two men who were found under the altar, a barber and an invalid, who had hidden there with a gimlet, to look up the women's petticoats, and who were hanged for it by the crowd. It is an absurd and vile little thing. It has been made, by the Hôtel de Ville, into a reason. They have hoisted the red flag. They have proclaimed martial law.
Lafayette has been delayed at the Hôtel de Ville by the quarrel in the Assembly, and he comes onto the field at nine, in the long pale green twilight of a July night, with the Guard, in a hollow square, with their bayonets fixed, and the drum beating. The crowd is perhaps six thousand, by now, thinning, with the lanterns among them. A few of them throw stones.
It is a very bad moment. You can feel what is going to happen as one can feel a storm in a tooth. There is a great deal that you could do, and perhaps nothing that would be any good.
:the_choice
* Go in over the bank, and bring Baptiste out by the collar
  $ b3.champ = "fetched"
  $ humanity += 2
  $ bond.baptiste += 2
  $ standing -= 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ do discover("the_champ_de_mars")
  You go down the slope in a run, through the crowd, which is beginning to sway, and up the four steps of the altar, two at a time, and take him by the back of the collar as one takes a puppy. He is very angry. He is a good deal stronger than he looks, and he turns on you in a fury, with the roll of paper clutched to his chest, and he says that it is a free country. You do not argue. You say, quite quietly, in his ear, that it is not a free country this afternoon. You get him down the steps, and through the thinning edge of the field, by a gap in the hurdles, into the dark of the elms of the Gros-Caillou, as the first volley goes off. It is high. It is a high volley, into the air, over the crowd, over the altar, and the whole of the field goes up in a roar of white doves, and dust, and one long wail. Then the second one is not.
* Stand between the Guard and the altar, with your hands open
  $ b3.champ = "stood"
  $ humanity += 3
  $ standing += 2
  $ secrecy -= 3
  $ injury += 2
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_champ_de_mars")
  You do it without thinking. You walk across the dust, in the last of the twilight, to the place at the foot of the altar steps, where the first rank of the battalion has come up in line, and you turn round with your back to the petition, and open your hands. It is a thing that you have not done since Etruria. A sergeant with a gray mustache sees you. It is Aubry. He does not lower the musket. He looks at you along it, with his old, shrewd, tired blue eyes, as along a road. There is a long second. And then somebody at the back gives the order, and the whole rank fires, and the line of smoke runs along it like a rope, and you are hit twice in the chest, and once in the shoulder, in front of three thousand people, and do not fall. It is the most remarkable thing that has ever happened on that field, and everyone sees it, and nobody says it. The Veil is a thing of the dark. It is dark, nearly. It is not quite dark enough.
* Stay away, and watch from the roof of the shop
  $ b3.champ = "stayed"
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing -= 1
  $ bond.baptiste -= 1
  $ do discover("the_champ_de_mars")
  You are on the roof, among the chimney pots, in the last green of the sky, with a good view over the whole of the southwest of the city, to the river and the long yellow line of the field. It is a long way. The sound arrives a little after the sight of it. It is a small, flat, dry, crackling sound, like the noise of someone walking over a field of stubble. A long white cloud lifts and spreads. You are aware that you have not moved. You are aware that you are holding on to a chimney. It is not, you think, that you were afraid. It is that you have found out what it is to be a man who watches.
The firing goes on for perhaps a minute. There are fifty dead, by the best account, and perhaps four hundred wounded, though nobody will ever count. The crowd goes out over the banks in a flood, screaming, and leaves a great number of small things lying in the dust: a hat, a shoe, a child's wooden horse, a Sunday parasol. The red flag is taken down. The altar stands in the middle of it all, empty, white, with a roll of paper on the third step, trodden into the dust, with four thousand names.
