## The eighteenth
Baptiste comes in at midnight. He is not dead. He is not even hurt. He has his coat torn, and a graze on the cheek, and he comes in through the back, quite quietly, and sits down at the case, and puts both his hands flat on the type, and begins to cry, without any noise, like a child.
?if b3.champ == "fetched"
  Nobody says anything about the collar. Adélaïde puts a basin of water in front of him. Her hand, on the edge of it, is not at all steady.
?elif b3.champ == "stood"
  Aubry comes later. He does not come in. He stands in the yard in the dark with his musket slung and his hat in his hand, and says, across the cobbles, in a flat voice, that he put his own round into the dust at your feet. He says that it was the only honest thing that he did that day, and that he did it too late to matter. He says that he is going to ask to be relieved. He is looking at your shoulder. You have put on a clean shirt, and a dark coat. He does not ask. He says that he has been thirty years in the service, and has seen a good many things that he was told, and has never till today been shown one.
?else
  Baptiste does not say where he was. Adélaïde does not ask. Nobody asks. It is a long supper.
?end
The Cordeliers are shut by the morning. The red flag has been taken down. The Jacobins, in a panic, have split, and the better part of the members have gone out to a new club, in a convent of the Feuillants, which is not to be republican. The petition is called, in the Assembly, a conspiracy. It will be said, for a long time, in the faubourg Saint-Antoine, that the king was never forgiven, and the nation was never the same, and the national Guard fired on the people. It will be said by those who were there.
There is a card on the counter, in the morning, in the Prince's own hand, in a grave, cold, upright script, that does not belong to Ottaviano. It is brief. It says that the Prince would be glad of a word with you at the Hôtel de Saint-Aubin on the next dark of the moon, and that it is a matter of some urgency, and that it concerns a lady.
Ottaviano has put a line at the foot.
?if b3.vault == "broke"
  It says that the slate has stopped. It says that the hand lies open on her knee. It says that, for the first time in four hundred years, nobody in the house can say what is to be done on the morrow, and that the Prince has not slept.
?elif b3.vault == "read"
  It says that the slate has stopped. It says that the last line was written on the evening of the sixteenth, and that it was your name, and a date, and nothing else, and that the date is not yet.
?else
  It says that the slate has stopped. It says that the hand has not moved since the sixth hour of the evening of the sixteenth. It says that he has gone down, in the night, and listened, and that the third cellar is the quietest place in Paris.
?end
There is a cup of coffee on the corner of the counting room desk, cooling, in a saucer. Adélaïde has set it there. She has not said what it is for.
